To find out when all of them will be adopted, you should divide the total number of puppies (2 + 34) by the number of puppies adopted per day:

\boxed{\boxed{60} = \boxed{\frac{\text{Total Puppies}}{\text{Puppies Adopted per Day}} }}

Since each day has 24 hours, you can divide the total time by 24:

\boxed{\boxed{\frac{60}{4}} = \boxed{\frac{15}{24}} }

Now, set up a fraction equivalent to 1 using the obtained fractions, by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the same number, until they are divisible by 24:

Multiply both the numerator and the denominator by 2:

\boxed{\boxed{\frac{15 \times 2}{24 \times 2}} }

This equation is now equivalent to 1:

\boxed{\boxed{\frac{30}{48}} = \boxed{\frac{1}{1}} }

Since the right-hand side of the equation is already in reduced form, the multiplication factors cancel out, and you find that the fraction itself is equal to 0.625:

\boxed{\boxed{0.625} = \boxed{\frac{30}{48}} }

Now, you have the fraction representing the time it takes to adopt all the puppies:

\boxed{\frac{30}{48} }

Since the number of days is equal to the time expressed in fractions of a day, you just need to convert this fraction into days:

$30 \div 8 = 3.75$ days

To get the number of days as an integer, you can round up to the nearest whole number to account for the remaining 0.75 fraction of a day:

\boxed{\boxed{\text{Answer}: 4 } }

So, it will take a total of \boxed{\text{4 days}} for all of the puppies to be adopted.

